### What is the Maximum Ribbon Cut problem?

Given a ribbon of length N and a set of allowed cut lengths, cut the ribbon into the maximum number of pieces where each piece must be one of the allowed lengths. Each allowed length can be used unlimited times. If the ribbon cannot be cut exactly using the allowed lengths, return -1.

```java
package com.bank.cheque;

public class MaximumRibbonCut {

    public static int maxPieces(int ribbonLength, int[] allowedLengths) {
        int[] dp = new int[ribbonLength + 1];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, Integer.MIN_VALUE);
        dp[0] = 0;

        for (int len = 1; len <= ribbonLength; len++) {
            for (int cut : allowedLengths) {
                if (cut <= len && dp[len - cut] != Integer.MIN_VALUE) {
                    dp[len] = Math.max(dp[len], 1 + dp[len - cut]);
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[ribbonLength] == Integer.MIN_VALUE ? -1 : dp[ribbonLength];
    }
}
```

### Why is this the maximization sibling of Minimum Coin Change?

Minimum Coin Change asks for the fewest coins that sum to a target. Maximum Ribbon Cut asks for the most pieces that sum to a length. Both use an unbounded supply and a sentinel for unreachable values, but one minimizes and the other maximizes. The sentinel and reachability guard are shared mechanics; only the optimization direction differs.

```java
// Minimum Coin Change: dp[len] = min(dp[len], 1 + dp[len - coin])
//   Sentinel: Integer.MAX_VALUE (unreachable = infinitely expensive)
//   Answer: dp[target] or -1 if still sentinel

// Maximum Ribbon Cut: dp[len] = max(dp[len], 1 + dp[len - cut])
//   Sentinel: Integer.MIN_VALUE (unreachable = impossible)
//   Answer: dp[N] or -1 if still sentinel
```

### How does the reachability guard work?

Before extending from dp[len - cut], you must check that dp[len - cut] is not the sentinel. If it is the sentinel, that remainder length is unreachable, and building on it would count an impossible cut. The guard `dp[len - cut] != Integer.MIN_VALUE` ensures you only extend from valid states.

```java
package com.bank.cheque;

public class ReachabilityDemo {

    public static void trace() {
        int ribbonLength = 7;
        int[] cuts = {3, 5};
        // dp[0]=0, dp[1]=MIN, dp[2]=MIN (unreachable with 3 and 5)
        // dp[3] = max(MIN, 1+dp[0]) = 1  (one piece of length 3)
        // dp[4] = check dp[1] for cut=3: MIN → skip
        //         check dp[-1]: skip (cut=5 too big)
        //         dp[4] = MIN (unreachable!)
        // dp[5] = max(MIN, 1+dp[2]=MIN → skip, 1+dp[0]=1) = 1
        // dp[6] = max(MIN, 1+dp[3]=2) = 2  (two pieces of length 3)
        // dp[7] = check dp[4]=MIN → skip for cut=3
        //         check dp[2]=MIN → skip for cut=5
        //         dp[7] = MIN → return -1!
        System.out.println("Ribbon 7 with cuts [3,5]: " +
            MaximumRibbonCut.maxPieces(7, cuts)); // -1
    }
}
```

```text
Ribbon 7 with cuts [3,5]: -1
```

### How does the DP table fill step by step?

For a ribbon of length 9 with allowed cuts [2, 3, 5], the DP fills as follows:

```java
package com.bank.cheque;

public class DPTrace {

    public static void trace() {
        int[] cuts = {2, 3, 5};
        int n = 9;
        // dp[0]=0
        // dp[1]=MIN (no cut fits)
        // dp[2]=max(MIN, 1+dp[0])=1 (one piece of 2)
        // dp[3]=max(MIN, 1+dp[1]=skip, 1+dp[0]=1)=1 (one piece of 3)
        // dp[4]=max(MIN, 1+dp[2]=2)=2 (two pieces of 2)
        // dp[5]=max(MIN, 1+dp[3]=2, 1+dp[2]=2, 1+dp[0]=1)=2
        //   Wait: cut=5 gives 1+dp[0]=1, cut=2 gives 1+dp[3]=2, cut=3 gives 1+dp[2]=2
        //   dp[5]=2 (e.g., pieces of 2+3)
        // dp[6]=max(1+dp[4]=3, 1+dp[3]=2, 1+dp[1]=skip)=3 (three pieces of 2)
        // dp[7]=max(1+dp[5]=3, 1+dp[4]=3, 1+dp[2]=2)=3
        // dp[8]=max(1+dp[6]=4, 1+dp[5]=3, 1+dp[3]=2)=4 (four pieces of 2)
        // dp[9]=max(1+dp[7]=4, 1+dp[6]=4, 1+dp[4]=3)=4

        System.out.println("Max pieces for ribbon 9: " +
            MaximumRibbonCut.maxPieces(9, cuts)); // 4
    }
}
```

```text
Max pieces for ribbon 9: 4
```

### What happens when the ribbon cannot be cut exactly?

If no combination of allowed lengths sums to exactly N, dp[N] remains at the sentinel value Integer.MIN_VALUE. In that case, return -1 to indicate impossibility. This is the same pattern as Minimum Coin Change returning -1 when no coin combination reaches the target.

```java
package com.bank.cheque;

public class ImpossibleCase {

    public static void test() {
        // Ribbon length 7, allowed cuts [4, 6]
        // dp[4]=1, dp[6]=1, dp[8]=2, but dp[7]=MIN
        // No way to combine 4s and 6s to make exactly 7
        System.out.println(MaximumRibbonCut.maxPieces(7, new int[]{4, 6})); // -1

        // Ribbon length 0: trivially 0 pieces
        System.out.println(MaximumRibbonCut.maxPieces(0, new int[]{3, 5})); // 0
    }
}
```

```text
-1
0
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Aspect | Complexity | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(N * C) | N = ribbon length, C = number of allowed cuts |
| Space | O(N) | 1D DP array |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is initializing dp to all zeros instead of a negative sentinel. Without the sentinel, you cannot distinguish between a reachable length with zero pieces and an unreachable length. Another mistake is skipping the reachability guard, which lets you build on unreachable states and count impossible cuts.

```java
package com.bank.cheque;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: initialize dp to 0 instead of sentinel
    public static int wrongVersion(int n, int[] cuts) {
        int[] dp = new int[n + 1]; // all zeros!
        for (int len = 1; len <= n; len++) {
            for (int cut : cuts) {
                if (cut <= len) {
                    dp[len] = Math.max(dp[len], 1 + dp[len - cut]);
                    // BUG: dp[len-cut] might be 0 for an unreachable length
                    // This counts impossible cuts as if they were possible!
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n]; // Returns wrong positive result for impossible cases
    }

    // CORRECT: sentinel + reachability guard
    // See MaximumRibbonCut.maxPieces above
}
```

### How is this used in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the cheque book department cuts strips into the maximum number of valid cheque segments. Only certain segment lengths are approved (regulatory requirement), and each length can be reused. The department needs to know if a strip can be cut exactly, and if so, what is the maximum number of segments.

```java
package com.bank.cheque;

public class ChequeStripCutter {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int stripLength = 12;
        int[] approvedSegments = {3, 5};
        int maxSegments = MaximumRibbonCut.maxPieces(stripLength, approvedSegments);
        if (maxSegments == -1) {
            System.out.println("Strip of length " + stripLength + " cannot be cut exactly.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Maximum segments from strip " + stripLength + ": " + maxSegments);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Maximum segments from strip 12: 4
```
